::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which digital publication choice best fits students who need quick instructions for joining an online speech rehearsal?
{
~A. A long article about the value of rehearsal.#This does not fit the immediate audience need. Students need direct joining instructions, not an extended explanation.
~B. A decorative image with no link or steps.#This may look appealing, but it fails the purpose because the audience cannot use it to join the rehearsal.
~C. A full transcript of a past rehearsal.#This gives too much unrelated information. The format should help students act quickly.
=D. A short checklist with the link, time, and steps.#Correct. This format matches the audience need because it is clear, usable, and organized for quick action.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which choice best shows audience awareness in an email to families about a speech showcase?
{
~A. Use inside jokes from class.#This may confuse families. Audience awareness means selecting language that fits readers who were not in the classroom.
=B. Include a clear subject line, date, location, purpose, and contact information.#Correct. Families need practical event information presented in a respectful and easy-to-use way.
~C. Use vague wording so the message sounds casual.#Vague wording weakens communication. Families need clear details to understand the event and decide how to participate.
~D. Put the most important information at the end only.#This makes the message harder to use. Audience-aware organization places key details where readers can find them quickly.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
A group is publishing slides for a presentation on interview preparation. Which choice best supports the speaker?
{
=A. Use brief headings and visuals that organize the speaker's explanation.#Correct. Slides should guide the audience and support what the speaker says aloud.
~B. Put the entire script on each slide.#This overloads the audience and weakens the role of the speaker. Slides should support, not replace, speech.
~C. Remove all visuals so the audience only listens.#This may reduce clarity. Useful visuals can help the audience follow key ideas when they are chosen carefully.
~D. Use unrelated images to make the slides more colorful.#Unrelated images distract from the message. Visuals should support the purpose and audience understanding.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which design choice most supports accessibility?
{
~A. Tiny text and low contrast colors.#This makes the publication harder to read. Accessibility requires clear, readable design.
~B. Several animations on every line.#Too much animation can distract from the message. Design should support understanding, not create confusion.
~C. Hidden key details under decorative images.#This makes important information hard to find. Audience-aware design keeps key information visible.
=D. Readable text size, clear headings, and strong contrast.#Correct. These choices help the audience see, scan, and understand the digital publication.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Before selecting a digital format, what should the communicator identify first?
{
=A. The audience and what they need to know first.#Correct. Audience and priority information should guide format, tone, organization, and design.
~B. The most decorative template available.#Decoration should not guide the communication plan. The audience's needs and purpose should come first.
~C. The longest possible message.#Length is not the goal. A strong publication includes enough information in a clear, useful form.
~D. The newest platform regardless of audience access.#A platform is only effective if the audience can access and use it. Popularity alone is not enough.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which publication plan best fits a school announcement about a career workshop?
{
~A. A complicated webpage with several unrelated sections.#This creates unnecessary work for the audience. A workshop announcement should be direct and easy to scan.
=B. A clear digital flyer with benefits, date, time, location, and sign-up link.#Correct. This gives the audience the information needed to understand the value and take action.
~C. A paragraph with no title, date, or sign-up information.#This fails the practical purpose. The audience needs key details to decide and participate.
~D. A visual collage with no explanation.#This may be decorative, but it does not communicate the event information clearly.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Why should digital slides avoid placing every detail on one slide?
{
~A. It makes the speaker look too prepared.#Preparation is not the problem. The issue is whether the audience can process the information clearly.
~B. It prevents the speaker from using any evidence.#Crowded slides are not mainly about evidence use. The problem is overload and poor organization.
~C. It guarantees the audience will disagree.#Crowding can confuse an audience, but it does not guarantee disagreement.
=D. It can overload the audience and make the message harder to follow.#Correct. Slides should organize ideas so the audience can process the presentation step by step.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which tone best fits a digital invitation to community guests for a presentation showcase?
{
~A. Sarcastic and dismissive.#This tone would not fit community guests. It may damage trust and reduce clarity.
~B. Confusing and overly casual.#This does not support a public invitation. Guests need clear and respectful communication.
=C. Respectful, welcoming, and clear.#Correct. This tone fits the audience and purpose because it invites participation professionally.
~D. Secretive and vague.#This prevents the audience from understanding the event. A digital invitation should be clear and usable.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which choice best explains why platform matters?
{
=A. The platform affects how the audience accesses, reads, and uses the message.#Correct. Digital publication requires choosing a format that fits how the audience will receive and act on the information.
~B. The platform matters only if the message is unimportant.#This is incorrect. Platform matters for important messages because access and clarity affect communication.
~C. The platform should always be the newest option.#New does not always mean useful. The platform should fit the audience and purpose.
~D. The platform replaces the need for clear organization.#No platform replaces organization. The message still needs clear structure and audience-aware design.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which choice best shows visual support for a spoken presentation?
{
~A. Slides that contain unrelated pictures.#Unrelated pictures distract from the speaker's message. Visuals should help the audience understand the content.
=B. Slides with clear headings that match the speaker's main points.#Correct. Clear headings guide the audience and support the speaker's organization.
~C. Slides with no connection to the speech order.#This creates confusion. Visual support should follow the structure of the spoken message.
~D. Slides that hide the main idea.#This works against communication. Visuals should make key ideas easier to follow.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which question best checks audience awareness?
{
~A. Does this design include every color I like?#Personal preference is not the main measure. Audience awareness focuses on what helps the audience understand.
~B. Can I make this message longer?#Longer is not automatically clearer. The message should be complete and usable for the audience.
~C. Did I choose the same format as everyone else?#Similarity is not enough. The format must fit the audience, purpose, and context.
=D. What does this audience need to know, and how can they use it easily?#Correct. This question connects audience needs with clear digital communication choices.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which publication choice is least audience-aware?
{
~A. A flyer with a clear title and sign-up link.#This is audience-aware because it helps readers understand and act on the message.
~B. An email with date, time, location, and purpose near the top.#This supports the audience by making essential information easy to find.
=C. A digital announcement with no purpose, no date, and no next step.#Correct. This fails audience awareness because readers cannot understand what to do or why the message matters.
~D. A slide with short headings and readable contrast.#This supports audience understanding because it is organized and visually clear.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
How should visuals and speaker notes work together?
{
=A. Visuals should guide attention while speaker notes explain details aloud.#Correct. This balance helps the audience follow the message without crowding the digital product.
~B. Visuals should include the entire speech so notes are unnecessary.#This overloads the audience and weakens the speaker's role. Notes should guide spoken explanation.
~C. Speaker notes should ignore the visuals.#This creates a disconnect. Notes and visuals should support the same communication purpose.
~D. Visuals should be unrelated so the audience stays surprised.#Surprise is not the goal. Visuals should help the audience understand the presentation.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which choice best revises a cluttered digital flyer?
{
~A. Add more fonts to make each line different.#More fonts often create clutter. A clear flyer uses consistent design to support reading.
~B. Move the date and sign-up link into tiny text.#This hides essential information. Audience-aware publication keeps action details visible.
~C. Add unrelated images to fill empty space.#Unrelated images distract from the purpose. Empty space can help readability.
=D. Use clear sections for benefits, date, location, and sign-up steps.#Correct. Clear sections organize the message so the audience can scan and act.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which statement best connects tone to audience?
{
~A. Tone should be the same for every audience.#This ignores audience awareness. Tone should fit the audience, purpose, and communication setting.
=B. Tone should match the relationship, purpose, and level of formality needed by the audience.#Correct. Audience-aware tone helps the message feel appropriate and understandable.
~C. Tone matters only in printed communication.#Tone matters in digital communication too because word choice affects how the audience receives the message.
~D. Tone should always be humorous.#Humor may not fit every audience or purpose. Tone should serve the communication goal.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
What is the best reason to use brief slide headings?
{
~A. They make the presentation look finished even if ideas are unclear.#Headings cannot replace clear ideas. They should support organization and understanding.
~B. They let the speaker skip transitions.#Headings can support transitions, but the speaker still needs to guide the audience.
=C. They help the audience follow the speaker's organization.#Correct. Brief headings act as signposts so listeners can track the presentation.
~D. They make the presentation longer.#Length is not the goal. Headings support clarity and structure.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which digital publication choice best supports a mixed audience of families, students, and staff?
{
=A. Clear language, essential details, and organization that does not assume background knowledge.#Correct. A mixed audience needs accessible wording and clearly organized information.
~B. Class-only abbreviations that students understand.#This may exclude families and staff. Audience-aware publication should not rely on insider language.
~C. A message that gives details only to one audience group.#This leaves other audience members without the information they need.
~D. A design with important details hidden in images.#This can reduce accessibility and clarity. Essential information should be readable and easy to find.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which choice best explains audience-aware organization?
{
~A. Put information in the order the creator thought of it.#This may not match audience needs. Organization should help the audience understand and act.
=B. Place the most important information where the audience can find it quickly.#Correct. Audience-aware organization prioritizes usability and clarity.
~C. Hide the main point to create suspense.#Suspense is not appropriate for most professional digital publications. The audience needs clarity.
~D. Use the same order for every communication product.#Different audiences and purposes may require different organization.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which slide choice best supports speaker credibility?
{
~A. A slide with unreadable text and no clear point.#This hurts credibility because the audience cannot follow the message.
~B. A slide full of unrelated jokes.#This distracts from the presentation purpose and may weaken the speaker's credibility.
=C. A slide with a clear claim, relevant visual, and organized support.#Correct. Clear and relevant design helps the audience trust and follow the speaker.
~D. A slide that contradicts what the speaker says.#This creates confusion and damages credibility.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which decision best matches purpose and platform?
{
~A. Use a long video when the audience only needs a deadline.#This does not fit the purpose. A deadline should be easy to find quickly.
~B. Use a hidden link when students need to sign up.#This creates a barrier. The platform should make action steps easy.
=C. Use a short announcement with a visible deadline and sign-up link.#Correct. This matches the purpose because the audience needs quick information and a clear next step.
~D. Use a complex format because it looks advanced.#Complexity is not the goal. The format should support understanding and action.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which choice best revises a message for a professional audience?
{
=A. Replace vague slang with clear, respectful, specific language.#Correct. Professional audiences need language that is clear, appropriate, and easy to act on.
~B. Add more jokes and remove the event details.#This weakens clarity and may not fit the setting. Professional tone should support the purpose.
~C. Use abbreviations only classmates know.#This excludes audience members who do not share the same background knowledge.
~D. Make the message less specific so it sounds relaxed.#Professional communication should be specific enough for the audience to understand and respond.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
What should a speaker do if a visual is interesting but unrelated to the message?
{
~A. Keep it because visuals are always helpful.#This is incorrect. Visuals help only when they support the message and audience understanding.
=B. Remove or replace it with a visual that supports the point.#Correct. A presentation visual should clarify or reinforce the message, not distract from it.
~C. Add several more unrelated visuals.#This increases distraction. More visuals do not improve communication if they do not fit the purpose.
~D. Stop explaining the point aloud.#The speaker still needs to explain ideas clearly. Visuals are support, not a replacement for communication.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which choice best describes an effective digital publication?
{
~A. It is designed mainly to show the creator's favorite style.#Personal style should not be the main goal. The publication should serve audience, purpose, and message.
~B. It includes as much information as possible without organization.#This overloads the audience. Effective publication uses structure and selection.
=C. It presents the message clearly through a format, organization, and design that fit the audience.#Correct. Effective digital publication connects format, structure, and design to audience needs.
~D. It avoids all visuals and headings.#Visuals and headings can support clarity when they are relevant and accessible.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which statement best explains why "audience first" matters?
{
~A. It lets the communicator ignore the purpose.#Audience and purpose work together. Knowing the audience helps the communicator fulfill the purpose.
~B. It means the communicator should only choose popular formats.#Popularity is not enough. The format must fit how the audience will use the message.
~C. It removes the need for revision.#Audience awareness often requires revision. The communicator checks clarity, tone, and organization.
=D. It helps the communicator choose language, organization, and design that the audience can understand and use.#Correct. Audience-first planning shapes practical choices throughout the digital publication.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L06_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L06_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which final check best supports a digital presentation product?
{
=A. Ask whether each visual, heading, and note supports the speaker's purpose and the audience's understanding.#Correct. This check connects design to presentation purpose and audience needs.
~B. Ask whether the product uses every available design feature.#Using every feature can create clutter. The best features are the ones that support the message.
~C. Ask whether the product hides the main point until the end.#This can confuse the audience. Presentation support should make the message easier to follow.
~D. Ask whether the product is longer than classmates' products.#Length does not prove quality. Clear audience-aware support is more important than size.
}
